{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002089","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 9 candidates are direct matches to ankylosing spondylitis by trait_efo and phenotyping_reported, so a direct-match selection is possible. However, most candidates come from framework-style UK Biobank sweeps ("across 813 traits" snpnet; "Portability of 245 polygenic scores") or have limited/less comparable performance reporting, which lowers certainty.

Among the EUR-anchored options, PGS002089 (LDpred2; 22,026 variants; UKB-derived, evaluated in EUR with additional GME/SAS) is preferred over PGS001267/PGS001268 because the latter are very sparse (10 variants) and from the pan-trait sparse UKB framework that domain guidance flags as often underperforming in external validation. PGS002089 is also favored over PGS001876 because it shows a higher reported partial correlation (0.0919 vs 0.0797) within the same publication family and endpoint, and uses a genome-wide shrinkage method with substantially more variants.

Main limitation: PGS002089 lacks PRS-comparable AUROC/R² (only partial-r is reported), and the seemingly strong AUROCs in the EAS-only Taiwanese models (PGS003420–PGS003424) are full-model AUROCs with unknown covariates and small validation (n=1,298), making them hard to compare and not enough to clearly displace the best-supported EUR-oriented choice without knowing the intended deployment ancestry. Therefore the best direct-match choice is identified, but overall evidence is not strong enough for a high-quality designation."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003428",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 6 candidates are direct matches (trait_efo=cervical carcinoma; phenotyping_reported is cervical cancer/incident cervical cancer). Among them, PGS003428 is from a cervical-phenotype GWAS meta-analysis focused on cervical phenotypes and risk stratification, uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (LDpred) with very large variant count (2,894,555), and reports a moderate per-SD effect size (HR=1.33) with only mild covariates (age, smoking), which is comparatively cleaner and more PRS-informative than models whose only reported discrimination is full-model AUROC/C-index with broader covariate bundles. However, the evidence remains limited because none of the candidates provide clearly comparable PRS-only discrimination in the key direct-match set (only PGS001299 reports PGS-only AUROC/R2, but it comes from a pan-trait UKB framework paper and shows weak PRS-only performance), and the full-model metrics reported across other models (e.g., AUROC 0.745) are not reliable for ranking standalone PRS quality. This makes the selection plausible but not high-certainty."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003382","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All five candidates are direct matches to cutaneous melanoma by trait_efo and broadly consistent trait/phenotyping labels. None reports clearly PRS-only discrimination (top-level performance_metrics.auc/r2 are null across models), so selection must lean on endpoint fidelity, covariate comparability, and the strongest available (but mostly full-model) performance evidence.

PGS003382 has a clean, diagnosis-like endpoint label ("skin cutaneous melanoma"), uses only basic covariates (age, sex, top 20 PCs), and provides the highest reported AUROC among the set (0.682) plus an R² value (0.0261), albeit not explicitly PRS-only; it also has a much larger variant count (672) than the GWAS-hit style scores, which is a supportive structural signal.

Main limitation: the reported AUROC/R² appear to be full-model metrics (not explicitly PRS-only), making cross-model comparability imperfect; additionally, PGS004247 and PGS003745 show strong per-SD effect sizes (HR 1.78; OR 1.49) but lack comparable AUC/R² and have less transparent/less clean covariate or study framing (e.g., unknown covariates for PGS003745), leaving residual uncertainty. Therefore the best choice is supported but not decisively high-quality on the visible evidence."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005218",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates exist for hypothyroidism, but PGS005218 is best supported by the visible evidence because it targets incident hypothyroidism and comes from a disease-focused GWAS/PRS paper specifically on hypothyroidism (Nat Genet, 2025), with very large multi-cohort training (n=1,146,562) and large validation (n=441,692). It also reports strong discrimination in the provided metrics (full-model AUROC 0.859), outperforming other candidates with reported AUROC (e.g., PGS004935 AUROC 0.70; PGS000965/PGS001181 full-model AUROC ~0.767; PGS000820 AUROC 0.64). Main limitation: the reported AUROC for PGS005218 is a full-model metric with thyroid biomarkers included as covariates (TSH, T4, anti-TPO), and no PGS-only AUROC/R2 is shown here; per the domain guidance, biomarker-adjusted full-model AUROC is not directly comparable to PRS-only performance, which lowers certainty despite the strong endpoint alignment and disease-focused study design."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005218",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates for hypothyroidism are present. PGS005218 is best supported in this context because it targets an aligned endpoint (phenotyping_reported: incident hypothyroidism), comes from a disease-focused hypothyroidism GWAS/prediction study (Nat Genet), has very large multi-cohort training (n=1,146,562 across multiple biobanks) and substantial validation (n=441,692), and reports strong discrimination (full-model AUROC 0.859; note covariates include thyroid biomarkers TSH/T4/anti-TPO). The main limitation is that the headline AUROC is a full-model metric with biomarker covariates that are near-outcome and can inflate performance; no PRS-only AUROC/R2 is provided, reducing comparability versus alternatives like PGS000965/PGS001181 that report PGS-only metrics but arise from a pan-trait UKB framework, and versus several other direct-match models that lack comparable PRS-only metrics. Because of this covariate-driven metric concern and incomplete PRS-only performance reporting, the selection is direct-match but not unequivocally high-quality."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005218",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates exist for hypothyroidism, but they vary in endpoint fidelity. Several are explicitly therapy-induced hypothyroidism in cancer patients (PGS000759/PGS000761/PGS000820), which is a treatment-specific endpoint mismatch versus generic hypothyroidism. Several others are from pan-trait or portability/framework-style papers (e.g., PLoS Genet '813 traits' snpnet models PGS000965/PGS001181; UKB portability models PGS001816/PGS002024), which domain guidance indicates tend to underperform disease-focused models when a strong disease-focused alternative exists. PGS005218 is a disease-focused GWAS/meta-analysis PRS for incident hypothyroidism with very large validation (n=441,692) and reports a high full-model AUROC (0.859), making it the strongest directly aligned, disease-focused candidate in the provided set. Main limitation: its reported AUROC is full-model and includes thyroid biomarkers (TSH, T4, anti-TPO), which the domain guidance flags as near-outcome covariates that inflate AUROC and are not PRS-comparable; no PGS-only AUROC/R2 or per-SD OR/HR is provided here to isolate the PRS contribution, so confidence is moderated despite the strong endpoint and study archetype."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 returned models are direct matches to the target concept “nodular goiter” via either nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) or benign nodular goiter (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273, PGS005274) are contrast endpoints (thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter) and are less directly aligned to generic nodular goiter status than the pure benign/nontoxic goiter endpoints.

Among the most phenotype-aligned candidates, PGS005262 is preferred because it targets “Benign nodular goiter” (closest surface match to ‘nodular goiter’), comes from a disease-focused multi-biobank thyroid-disease study family (medRxiv: “Global multi-ancestry genetic study…”), uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRSCS) with a very large variant set (~1.09M), and shows the strongest reported discrimination among the benign-goiter models in the same family (full-model AUROC 0.593 vs 0.585 for PGS005261 and 0.588 for PGS005263) along with a relatively strong per-SD effect size (OR 1.41). 

Main limitation: performance evidence is not clearly PRS-only (reported AUROC is full-model and covariates are “Unknown”), reducing comparability and preventing a high-confidence choice; additionally, the nontoxic multinodular goiter UKB portability models provide only weak partial-r (~0.024–0.028) and appear framework/portability-derived, but they do have explicit basic covariates and multi-ancestry evaluation, making the overall ranking non-decisive."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003861","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to pulmonary embolism by trait_efo, with varying endpoint specificity. Among the EUR UKB pan-trait framework snpnet scores (PGS001277/78/79/80; publication: "Significant sparse polygenic risk scores across 813 traits in UK Biobank"), several have PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC ~0.59–0.61; PGS-only R2 ~0.011–0.015) and basic covariates, but this study archetype is explicitly flagged in the provided domain knowledge as a pan-trait framework whose sparse scores often generalize poorly, lowering confidence despite complete metrics; additionally, some endpoints are composite (PE +/- DVT; DVT or lung clot) or time-to-event (PGS001280), which are slight endpoint-alignment penalties versus a straightforward PE diagnosis.

PGS003861 is disease-focused (Han Chinese PE GWAS in BMC Med) with an exact PE phenotype label and reports a strong effect size (OR 5.08 with CI) plus AUROC=0.765 (not PRS-only; full-model), providing the most supportive disease-specific signal among the candidates. However, its covariates are unknown and the only AUROC reported is full-model (domain knowledge: do not substitute full-model AUROC for PRS-only), and it is EAS-only development/evaluation, so transportability to non-EAS settings is uncertain from the visible evidence. The newer EUR UKB models (PGS004460/PGS004530) are exact ICD10 I26 endpoints and use strong method families (LDpred2; metaPRS), but they lack PRS-comparable performance metrics here (only a single OR without per-SD definition and no AUROC/R2), making them hard to justify as best-supported in this context.

Therefore, a direct-match exists, but the best choice remains sub-optimal due to non-comparable/missing PRS-only metrics and ancestry/covariate uncertainties across the top candidates; PGS003861 is selected as the best-supported based on disease-focused endpoint alignment and strong reported association signal."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for the umbrella target (uterine carcinoma) include one exact-label model (PGS001795; trait_efo=uterine carcinoma; phenotyping_reported=uterine cancer) and multiple clinically dominant-subtype models labeled endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma (PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002735/736/737, PGS003381, PGS004244; all trait_efo=endometrial carcinoma). Domain knowledge in context states endometrial carcinoma is the dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma (~90%), so high-quality endometrial-cancer endpoints are acceptable direct matches.

Among these, PGS003381 has strong endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported explicitly “uterine endometrial carcinoma”), uses a genome-wide method (lassosum) with a large variant set (529,365), and reports supportive evaluation metrics (AUROC 0.761; R² 0.11) with relatively clean covariates (age + top 20 PCs). Competing endometrial models are either sparse GWAS-hits scores (e.g., PGS000075/000786 with only 9 variants and covariates including family history and multiple clinical/epidemiologic factors; PGS002735/736/737 with low AUROCs ~0.53–0.56 and unknown covariates) or have limited reported metrics (PGS004244 reports only HR). The exact-label uterine cancer model PGS001795 is from a global biobank framework paper and shows weaker PRS-comparable signal (pgs_only_r2 0.001948; AUROC 0.66), which does not outweigh the stronger dominant-subtype, higher-structure evidence for PGS003381.

Main limitation: performance metrics across candidates are not consistently PRS-only/comparable (many are full-model AUROCs), and external validation details for PGS003381’s training are sparse in the provided metadata, so confidence is moderated despite a clearly preferable evidence package."}
